{
  "image_name": "8f36048dc99833503fb10c5b42ad56dc.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a reversible liquid organic hydrogen carrier cycle based on GBL and BDO using an inverse Al2O3/Cu catalyst. At the center, the catalyst drives two opposite reactions: hydrogenation converts the hydrogen-lean compound GBL into the hydrogen-rich compound BDO, while dehydrogenation converts BDO back to GBL.\n\nThe top half shows hydrogen uptake. A gas mixture containing more hydrogen is fed in, and the scheme indicates addition of 2 H2 to GBL. This produces BDO and leaves behind a hydrogen-lean gas mixture, meaning hydrogen has been selectively stored in the liquid molecule. The process is labeled exothermal, releasing 43.2 kJ per mol H2.\n\nThe bottom half shows hydrogen release. BDO undergoes dehydrogenation, removing 2 H2 and generating GBL plus pure hydrogen gas. This reverse step is marked endothermal, requiring 43.2 kJ per mol H2.\n\nOverall, the graphic highlights a closed-loop hydrogen storage concept: hydrogen can be captured from mixed gas streams into a liquid carrier and later released as pure hydrogen, with the same catalyst enabling both directions.",
